    "https://naceweb.org/docs/default-source/default-document-library/2025/publication/research-report/nace-2026-job-outlook-report.pdf": {
      "title": "PUBLISHED NOVEMBER 2025",
      "description": "  PUBLISHED NOVEMBER 2025  \n2026\nJob Outlook\nThe annual Job Outlook survey is a forecast of employer’s hiring\nintentions of new college graduates. Data for the Job Outlook 2026\nsurvey were collected from August 7, 2025, through September 22,\n2025. Data are calculated based on the number of respondents to\neach specific question. Totals may not equal 100% due to rounding.\nNACE expects to provide a formal job market update during the \n2025-26 academic year. Based on data collected in February and\nMarch, the Job Outlook 2026 Spring Update report will provide \na final update on hiring for 2025-26 graduates. The results will be\navailable in early April.\nAbout the Job Outlook 2026 Survey\n2026 Job Projections for \nEntry-Level Hiring\nEmployers Rate Overall Job Market\n\nFall Spring Update\n2019 2020 2021 2022 2023 2024 2025 2026\n0%\n-10%\n0%\n10%\n20%\n30%\n40%\n16.6%\n10.7%\n5.8% 6.3%\n-0.1%\n7.2%\n26.6%\n31.6%\n14.7%\n3.9%\n-1.9%\n-5.8%\n7.3%\n0.6% 1.6%\nJob Outlook Hiring Projections, \n2019 - 2026\nJob Outlook Projections Class of 2026\n\n0% 20% 40% 60% 80%\nCommitment to succession planning/importance of talent pipeline\nCompany growth\nIncreased demand for our products and services\nAnticipated increase in the number of retirements\nOther\n72.7%\n68.2%\n29.5%\n31.8%\n2.3%\nKey Factors for Increasing Hires\n*Percentages do not equal 100, as respondents were able to select more than one factor.\nNUMBER OF RESPONDENTS = 44\nKey Factors for Decreasing Hires\n*Percentages do not equal 100, as respondents were able to select more than one factor.\nNUMBER OF RESPONDENTS = 26\nIndustries Hiring Changes\n\n2017 2018 2019 2020 2021 2022 2023 2024 2025 2026\n0%\n10%\n20%\n30%\n40%\n50%\n60%\n12.3%\n15.4%\n19.3% 20.2%\n34.8%\n40.7%\n51.6% 52.6% 52.7%\n48.2%\nRecruiting Year\nPercent of Respondents\nAssociate Degree Hiring,  \nby Percent of Respondents\nFall vs. Spring Recruiting for the \nClass of 2026, by Percent of Respondents\n\nOverall Positions\nFully Remote Fully Hybrid Fully In-Person\n2022 Spring Update 2023 Fall 2023 Spring Update 2024 Fall 2024 Spring Update 2025 Fall 2025 Spring Update 2026 Fall\n0%\n10%\n20%\n30%\n40%\n50%\n49%\n48% 45% 48% 50%\n47%\n42%41%\n39%\n36%\n39%\n41% 40% 42%\n43%\n48%\n20%\n15% 13% 14% 12%\n8% 10% 10%\nModality of Overall Job Positions,  \nby Percent of Respondents\nFully Remote Fully Hybrid Fully In-Person\n2022 Spring Update 2023 Fall 2023 Spring Update 2024 Fall 2024 Spring Update 2025 Fall 2025 Spring Update 2026 Fall\n0%\n10%\n20%\n30%\n40%\n50%\n60%\n40%\n50% 48% 48%\n51%\n54%\n49%\n44%\n42%\n38%\n42% 43% 41% 42%\n45%\n50%\n18%\n12% 10% 9% 8%\n4% 6% 6%\nModality of Entry-Level Job Positions, \nby Percent of Respondents\nBachelor's Master's\nIncrease salaries No change to salaries Decrease salaries\n0%\n20%\n40%\n60%\n80%\n40.3%\n28.3%\n59.7%\n71.7%\n0.0%       0.0%\nChange in Salaries for Bachelor’s \nand Master’s Degree Graduates\nPlanned Actual\n2020-21 2021-22 2022-23 2023-24 2024-25 2025-26\n0%\n10%\n20%\n30%\n40%\n50%\n60%\n50.0%\n51.8%\n47.7%\n50.0%\n52.8%\n48.8%\n52.9% 52.1%\n59.3%\n55.6%\nEmployers Offering Signing Bonuses\nSelected graduates\n52.8%\nAll graduates\n47.2%\nSigning Bonus Plans,  \nby Percent of Respondents\nSkills-Based Hiring and \nCollege Graduates\n2019 2020 2021 2022 2023 2024 2025 2026\n0%\n20%\n40%\n60%\n80%\nPercent of Respondents\nRecruiting Year\n73.3%\n63.0%\n56.6%\n46.3%\n37.0% 38.3% 38.1% 42.1%\nGPA Screening\n0% 20% 40% 60% 80% 100%\nAcademic major\nIndustry experience\nHas completed an internship within your industry\nHas completed an internship with your organization\nHas demonstrated proﬁciency in competencies\nHas held a leadership position\nHas been involved in extracurricular activities\nHas held part-time jobs while in college to ﬁnance education\nHas done volunteer work\nSchool's reputation\nHas foreign language and/or intercultural skills\nHas completed a study abroad program\nOther\n93.7%\n81.1%\n78.9%\n73.7%\n72.6%\n63.2%\n56.8%\n43.2%\n34.7%\n26.3%\n12.6%\n4.2%\n3.2%\nPercent of Respondents*\n*Percentages do not equal 100, as respondents were able to select more than one factor.\nAdditional Factors Considered by\nRespondents That Do Not Screen by GPA*\nPercent of Respondents*\n0% 20% 40% 60% 80% 100%\nAcademic major\nHas completed an internship within your industry\nIndustry experience\nHas held a leadership position\nHas completed an internship with your organization\nHas been involved in extracurricular activities\nHas demonstrated proﬁciency in competencies\nHas done volunteer work\nSchool's reputation\nHas foreign language and/or intercultural skills\nOther\nHas completed a study abroad program\n95.5%\n86.6%\n82.1%\n82.1%\n79.1%\n73.1%\n67.2%\n44.8%\n28.4%\n9.0%\n6.0%\n4.5%\n*Percentages do not equal 100, as respondents were able to select more than one factor.\nAdditional Factors Considered by\nRespondents That Do Screen by GPA*\n2025 2026\n0% 10% 20% 30% 40% 50% 60%\nOther\n23.2%\n30.4%\n50.8%\n43.5%\n24.3%\n21.7%\n1.7%\n4.3%\nANY majors regardless of whether \nthey relate to our specific industry\nMajors that are exclusive to our\nindustry AND other majors that fall\noutside the realm of our industry\nMajors that are only\nexclusive to our industry\nEmployers Consider  \nHiring All Majors\n69.5%\n30.5%\nYes No\nUse Skills-Based Hiring\n(N=164)\n0% 10% 20% 30% 40%\nAlways\nMost of the time\nAbout half the time\nSometimes\nNever\n18.4%\n37.7%\n14.9%\n25.4%\n3.5%\nNUMBER OF RESPONDENTS = 114\nSkills-Based Hiring Frequency\nNUMBER OF RESPONDENTS = 107\n0% 20% 40% 60% 80% 100%\nInterviewing\nScreening\nSourcing\nPost-hire placement/matching\n86.9%\n65.4%\n37.4%\n29.9%\n22.4%\nIncumbent worker promotions or\nmovement into new roles or areas\nStages Using\nSkills-Based Hiring\nNUMBER OF RESPONDENTS = 106\n0% 20% 40% 60% 80% 100%\nCompetency-based job descriptions\nInterview rubrics\nInternally-created assessment tool\nExternally-created assessment tool\nExternal recruiting agency\nDigital badges/microcredentials\nGame-based assessments\nOther\n81.1%\n57.5%\n35.8%\n24.5%\n12.3%\n4.7%\n4.7%\n2.8%\nSkills-Based \nHiring \nPractices\nBeing Used\n0% 10% 20% 30% 40% 50%\nNot challenging at all\nSlightly challenging\nModerately challenging\nVery challenging\nExtremely challenging\n24.3%\n46.7%\n26.2%\n1.9%\n0.9%\nNUMBER OF RESPONDENTS = 107\nLevel of Challenge\nin Implementing\nSkills-Based Hiring\nPractices\n0% 10% 20% 30% 40% 50%\nBuy-in from hiring managers\nCultural hurdles within organization\nLack of assessment tools\nRegulatory hurdles\nOther\n46.3%\n46.3%\n36.8%\n28.4%\n12.6%\n5.3%\nNUMBER OF RESPONDENTS = 95\nLack of department time/resources to\nimplement new hiring practices\nRoadblocks in Implementing Skills-Based Hiring\nNUMBER OF RESPONDENTS = 108\n0% 20% 40% 60% 80% 100%\nPrepare for interviews that demonstrate their skills\nParticipate in experiential learning and/or work during college\nTranslate college coursework into a skills language\nTranslate extracurricular activities into a skills language\nCreate a skills-based resume and/or cover letter\nObtain industry-recognized certiﬁcations, e.g. Google, Microsoft, etc.\nObtain career readiness skill certiﬁcations, e.g. communication, teamwork, etc.\nCreate a skills portfolio that demonstrates skills, accomplishments, and work samples\nOther\n88.9%\n74.1%\n61.1%\n54.6%\n50.0%\n38.9%\n30.6%\n26.9%\n0.9%\nMost Important Ways Students Can Demonstrate Skills\nDuring a Skills-Based Hiring Process\n*Percentages do not equal 100, as respondents were able to choose more than one learning opportunity.\n0% 20% 40% 60% 80% 100%\nU.S. based internships\nCo-ops\nOn-campus student work\nApprenticeships\nParticipation in faculty-led research projects\nExternships\nMicrointernships\nPracticum (or clinical) experiences\nInternational internships\nService learning\nInternational work experience\nStudy abroad\n97%\n76%\n43%\n41%\n36%\n29%\n28%\n25%\n15%\n15%\n13%\n8%\nMost Valuable Experiential Learning\nOpportunities on a Student’s Resume\n2026 Average Inﬂuence Rating*\n2025 Average Inﬂuence Rating*\n0 1 2 3 4 5\nHas completed an internship with your organization\nHas internship experience in your industry\nMajor\nHas general work experience\nHas held leadership position\nHigh GPA (3.0 or above)\nHas done volunteer work\nSchool attended\nIs ﬂuent in a foreign language\nHas international work experience\nHas studied abroad\n4.5\n4.4\n4.3\n4.3\n3.8\n4.0\n3.6\n3.8\n3.5\n3.4\n3.3\n3.4\n2.9\n3.0\n2.6\n2.6\n2.4\n2.5\n2.0\n2.1\n1.8\n1.7\n1.7\n*5-point scale, where 1=No influence at all, \n2=Not much influence, 3=Somewhat of an influence, \n4=Very much influence, and 5=Extreme influence.\nHas been involved in extracurricular activities\n(clubs, sports, student government, etc.)\nChoosing Between\nEqually Qualified\nCandidates - \nInfluence of Attributes\nSocial Media and Recruiting\n*Percentages do not equal 100, as respondents were able to select more than one platform.\n100%\n%\n0 20 40 60 80 100\nInstagram\n50.4%\nFacebook\n30.4%\nOther\n9.6%\n8.9%\nTik Tok\nX\n6.7%\nLinkedIn\n%\n%\n%\n%\n%\nNUMBER OF RESPONDENTS = 135\nSocial Media Platform Use,  \nby Percent of Respondents*\n0% 20% 40% 60% 80% 100%\nBuilding brand awareness\nAdvertising of positions\nNetworking with potential candidates\nSearching for candidates\nBuilding community\nChecking candidate proﬁles\nScreening candidates\n95.6%\n86.7%\n63.7%\n57.8%\n54.8%\n37.0%\n9.6%\nNUMBER OF RESPONDENTS = 135\nWays Early Talent Recruiters Use Social Media,  \nby Percent of Respondents\nAI and Entry-Level Jobs\nAI Skills\n13.3% of jobs require AI skills\n10.5% of entry-level jobs include \nAI in job descriptions\n0% 10% 20% 30% 40% 50% 60% 70%\n61.0%\n25.0%\n14.0%\n0.0%\n0.0%\nNot replacing\nUnsure if replacing\nHaving discussions about replacing\nReplacing in the next 5 years\nReplacing in the next 3 years\nReplacing Entry-Level Jobs With AI, \nby Percent of Respondents\n0% 5% 10% 15% 20% 25% 30% 35%\n31.4%\n27